 The bicycle has a long and fascinating history, dating back to the 19th century. The first bicycles were called "velocipedes" and were invented in the 1810s by German inventor Karl von Drais. These early models had no pedals and were propelled by the rider pushing their feet against the ground. In the 1830s, the "dandy horse" was developed, which had a wooden frame and two wheels, and was also propelled by foot power. The first bicycles with pedals were invented in the 1860s, and these were called "penny-farthings" due to their large front wheel and small rear wheel. The invention of the safety bicycle in the 1880s, with equal-sized wheels and a chain-driven system, made bicycles more stable and easier to ride, leading to their widespread popularity. Today, bicycles are a popular mode of transportation and recreation around the world.
